  - [1mA. Derived, no stored notifications (recommended).[0m "Needs attention" is computed from the tasks themselves at render
    time — a task is in the list because its due date says so, not because a record was created. Dismissal is a
    [38;5;153msnoozedUntil[39m field on the task. No notification entities to go stale, duplicate, or drift out of sync with the tasks
    they describe. Cheapest to build and the least to get wrong.
  - [1mB. Stored notification records.[0m A generator writes notification rows when a task crosses a threshold; the UI reads
    an inbox with read/unread history. Buys you a persistent activity history and per-item read state. Costs real
    bookkeeping — regeneration, deduplication, and cleanup when the underlying task changes or is deleted. Worth it only
    if you want history, which single-user rarely needs.
  - [1mC. Browser/OS notifications via service worker.[0m Fires even when the tab is closed. A genuine capability gain, not
    just a UI difference. Costs a permission prompt, a service worker, and unreliable delivery (the browser has to be
    running). You said in-page is fine, so I've set this aside — but it's the one option here that adds something A and
    B can't do, so say the word if "notify" meant OS-level to you.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. It gives you the outcome you asked for at roughly a tenth of the machinery, and B or C can be layered
  on later without undoing it.

  I'd also deliberately [1mcut[0m a change/event log for now. It's the natural seam for real notifications and for undo, but
  under single-user both of those are speculative, and I'd rather not build infrastructure on a maybe.

  I haven't written any code — checking this reframe before I present the actual design.
